INT. BONE-WHITE SALOON - WHALE RIB BUILDING - NOON

A saloon framed by the ribs of an enormous skeleton. Sunlight through bone. Sawdust. A faded mural of a harpooner.

MARSHAL TEAGUE (50s, tin star, sun-cracked skin) stands at the bar. OTTILIE (40s, apron, flour on her sleeves) pours coffee. LEFTY (30s, left hand missing, grin) sits at a corner table. DEPUTY NASH (20s, pressed shirt, jittery) watches the door.

TEAGUE
Ottilie, who's the stranger who came in at dawn?

OTTILIE
Said his name was Smith.

TEAGUE
Everybody's name is Smith.

LEFTY
Not mine.

TEAGUE
Yours is trouble, Lefty.

LEFTY
That's my middle name.

NASH
Marshal, he's across the street.

TEAGUE
Then let him stay there.

NASH
He's got a rifle.

OTTILIE
They all have rifles.

LEFTY
Don't look so scared, Deputy. He's more scared than you.

NASH
How do you know?

LEFTY
He's still sitting.

Teague sets his cup down. He draws his pistol, checks the chamber, holsters it. Nash swallows. Ottilie wipes her hands.

TEAGUE
Everyone down.

They duck behind the bar. A shot cracks through the window and kills the mirror. Glass rains. Lefty whoops.

Teague rises, fires once out the window. A body drops outside.

NASH
Did you...

TEAGUE
Wasn't him. That was his horse.

OTTILIE
Oh, Lord.

LEFTY
That's gonna be a long walk home.
